# Cloudflare Pages cache rules.
#
# The shell is never cached: it is the file that names which build's script to load, so a
# stale one is a dead page. Everything it names is content-addressed and immutable — the
# `.wasm` files by the Kotlin/Wasm toolchain, `composeApp.<hash>.js` by the deploy workflow —
# so a year is safe for those and nothing else.
#
# `no-store` rather than `no-cache` because the zone's Browser Cache TTL overrides a weaker
# origin header (§6c). This is belt and braces over the content hashing, which is the thing
# actually relied on; if these two ever disagree, the hash wins and this file is the bug.
/
  Cache-Control: no-store
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-store

/*.wasm
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/composeApp.*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# --- Compose resources -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **`immutable` was a lie here, and it corrupted the app on every returning visitor.**
# Nothing under `/composeResources/` carries a content hash — the paths are fixed names the
# wasm has baked into it — so promising a year of immutability promises something the URL
# cannot keep. The rule above for `/*.wasm` and `/composeApp.*.js` is fine precisely because
# those two *are* content-addressed.
#
# What made it more than a stale picture: Compose does not look strings up by key at runtime.
# The generated accessors carry a **byte offset and length** into
# `values*/strings.commonMain.cvr`, and those numbers live in the wasm. The wasm is
# content-hashed, so it is always this build's; the `.cvr` was `immutable`, so it was whatever
# build the visitor first loaded. Pair a new offset table with an old file and every string
# after the first changed entry is read from the wrong place: truncated mid-word, decoded as
# garbage, or empty. Strings *before* that entry are untouched, which is why the home screen
# looked perfect and everything behind "Play online" did not.
#
# Reproduced by deleting one entry from the deployed `.cvr` and loading the deployed bundle:
# blank buttons, "JOI" where "How to play" belongs, and mojibake in place of the version.
#
# So: the string tables are never stored, and everything else is bounded rather than forever.
/composeResources/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, must-revalidate

# The string tables, listed one locale at a time on purpose.
#
# Exact paths rather than a `values*` wildcard because a rule that silently fails to match is
# the whole failure mode above, wearing a different hat — and `no-store` rather than
# `no-cache` for the same reason `/` uses it: the zone's Browser Cache TTL overrides a weaker
# origin header (§6c), and "weaker" includes a revalidation hint. They are 104 KB together
# against 13.7 MB of wasm that *is* cached, so this costs nothing worth counting.
#
# `WebShellTest.everyStringTableIsRefetchedAndRepaired` fails if a locale is added to
# `commonMain/composeResources` without a line here and a matching one in `index.html`.
/composeResources/game.vinto.app.art/values/strings.commonMain.cvr
  Cache-Control: no-store

/composeResources/game.vinto.app.art/values-ru/strings.commonMain.cvr
  Cache-Control: no-store
